A||||9||BBBAD GT||Upgrading tires/rims...need quick help/opinons||||6||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||02-28-2000||02:08 PM||||I'm getting a hell of a deal on some 16x7 rims (with 35mm offset).  My question is out to all who have upgraded tire sizes, I'm thinking of going with 205/55-16 up front, and 225/50-16 out back Dunlop SP sport 8000's, should I go with a slightly bigger tire up front to 215, or should I leave well enough alone at 205 because the steering is still pretty heavy.<P>Thanks for the opinions/responses.   <P>These things are going to look killer on the GT......||||reg||6
Z||000001||batboy||02-28-2000||03:07 PM||||205 up front and 225 in the rear ought to be perfect.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||02-28-2000||11:13 PM||||Anything larger than a 205 in the front will take a very long time to heat up causing  your car to push and have generaly crappy responce. Theres not enough weight up there to heat up anything else... I only run wider tires cause thats what fits on my rims. As for the rear whats that Pontiac commercial  "wider is better"  225 should heat up well with a 6 romping on them both on the track and around town. And a 225-45 will only toss your speed o off .08 mph @ 60 225*.45/25.4*2+16=23.97 that same formula with a 215-60-14 (stock on my car sorry thats my only reference) gives us 24.15 divde 23.97/24.15=.9925  or .08 @ 60 mph. The Dunlop 8000s ROCK! kinda slick when their cold but a few minutes on the road and your able to just FLOG 'em! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||BBBAD GT||03-01-2000||09:56 AM||||Here is the deal I got, if anybody is interested.<P>$65/ea  16x7 MIM Vento 35mm offset<BR>$88/ea  205/55-ZR16 Dunlop SP 8000<BR>$84/ea  225/50-ZR16 Dunlop SP 8000<BR>$42 lugs/locks/mounting/balancing<BR>$104 Shipping<P>$750  Total<P>Not bad in my opinion, the reason the wheels were so cheap, they were discontinued and there was only one set left at 5x100mm 16x7.  Sorry about that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>This was at  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com/discount.splash.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.discounttiredirect.com/discount.splash.html</A> <P>Here is a pic of the wheel:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.discounttiredirect.com/sebring/dtcweb/image/specials/vento.sp.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||03-06-2000||09:01 PM||||Hey, BBBAD, could you post a pic of your car when they're on? I've been looking at a ton of rims, and I tend to favor 5 spokes... just want to see 'em.<P>BTW, were the Dunlops your choice? Have we any consensus of tires? (I run BFG Comp T/A 205/60 front and 225/60 rear)||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||03-06-2000||10:51 PM||||Patrick Dad...   I had 5 spokes..    <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Big O||03-07-2000||09:47 AM||||BBad,  You got one heck of a deal on both the tires and the wheels.  Yes, your selection of sizes is right on.  The Dulop SP8000 is a great tire and I can't see why they discontinued them.  I've been trying to get my hands on a bunch of them for my tire shop but everyone seems to be out.  I think when Dunlop decided to discontinue them a few selected shops or dealers bought them all.  I was able to get my hands on one set of them through the Big O warehouse for dirt cheap and they went in a heartbeat.  When I went back for more they were all gone.  For those of you who are looking for wheels, take your time.  Most of the wheel companies copy each other to a certain extent and there are lots of wheels out there that look the same with price ranges from one end of the spectrum to the other.  Its best to look for a style first and then start shopping prices.  Aftermarket wheel sales are a very competitive business with very little markup.  If you want the best deal negotiate for tire and wheel packages with the dealer and ask for the wheels at the dealers cost.  To get the tire sale he will probably say yes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BBBAD GT||03-08-2000||12:01 AM||||Got the wheels/tires today, and I must say they look pretty darn good.  I'll have to borrow my bro-in-law's digital camera again and get some pics. <P>One question, with the stock suspension, the front looks good, with the outside fender about 1-1.5" above the tire.  But the rear is a different story, the fender is about 3-3.5" above the tire.  I'm thinking of cutting about a half coil from the rear springs to get it down to acceptable levels, has anybody tried cutting the stock springs?  Or have you guys just gone with lowering springs by Eibach or S/T?||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||03-08-2000||08:56 AM||||OH NO!!! please don't cut your springs...! In the long sad history of Ricey moves that ranks right up there at the top just under the fart can exhaust tip. You'll hate the ride and your springs have a better chance or popping out of their seats. THe Fiero store sells just the rear springs if your just wanting to drop the rear. You'll kick you self if you cut the springs.. I worked at NOPI for over a year and have seen and been in quite a few cars with cut springs... you've seen them their the ones with blown out struts and bouncing down the road head bashing on the sunroof! Don't do that to your new tires! it will cause uneven wear charactoristics cause as close as you may think you can cut them the same height it will never be right and not worth the trouble. 59014B part # for the rear springs at the Fiero Store $105 and you'll only have to do it once.   (oh and BBAD you asked about what my fuel flow was at 47psi... (20.4 lbhr))  I'll scan the flow bench test and post it up here for ya... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BBBAD GT||03-08-2000||09:55 AM||||hehe...I'll take your recommendation on not cutting the springs....I've always just replaced the spings in other cars...never delt with a Fiero suspension<P>I ran your 20.7lb injectors at 47psi through a calculation I had, and if those injectors are initially rated at 40 psi (most are), then you have a set of approx. 17lb injectors at 40 psi (this might be where you got the stock cobra rating, which is 24lb at 40psi).  The additional 7psi of fuel pressure is making them act like a 20.7lb injector.  And if you play on that theme, at 80psi, your injectors are acting like 35lb injectors.  <P>Also, 17lb injectors at 40 psi with a BSFC of .55 (higher for blown/turbo cars) could support roughly 220 hp (which is 13.4's at 101mph at 2700lb), 20.7lb inj at 47psi can support 255hp (which is 12.8's at 106 mph at 2700lb), and finally 35lb inj at 84 psi can support 450 hp (which is 10.5's at 128mph).  <P>Don't forget, with pressure and injectors, there is a point of deminishing returns.  You can't crank a 15lb injector to 150psi and think its (calculated) 55lb injectors.  I think around the 100psi mark, is where it starts to set in.  And also, don't forget which type of injector you have, some can support up to 100psi of pressure, some can't.  <P>Math lesson is over  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
